1
00:00:00,760 --> 00:00:03,560
They prowled the chilly depths of the ocean.

2
00:00:03,561 --> 00:00:07,360
Like wolves, they hunted in packs and picked off prey.

3
00:00:07,361 --> 00:00:11,320
And unleashed a special brand of mayhem on allied ships.

4
00:00:11,321 --> 00:00:17,120
They put a chokehold on England that nearly starved her in a submission twice in one century.

5
00:00:17,121 --> 00:00:20,000
Now, U-Boats, next on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,640
After more than 70 years, they are still being found.

7
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:42,600
The rusted remains of the deadliest submarine force in history.

8
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:49,540
For some, they were known as the Gray Wolves.

9
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:51,780
Others called them Steel Coffins.

10
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:55,260
But all came to respect the power of the U-boats.

11
00:00:55,261 --> 00:01:00,500
Today the ocean floor is home to the carnage of two world wars

12
00:01:00,501 --> 00:01:02,820
8,000 cargo ships

13
00:01:02,821 --> 00:01:06,540
And the skeletal remains of over 80,000 combatants

14
00:01:06,541 --> 00:01:09,660
Untouched for decades

15
00:01:09,661 --> 00:01:11,720
The twisted hulls of the German U-boats

16
00:01:11,721 --> 00:01:14,180
Belied the threat they once posed

17
00:01:14,181 --> 00:01:15,940
For twice in a generation

18
00:01:15,941 --> 00:01:18,640
They alone held the fate of Europe in their grasp

19
00:01:18,641 --> 00:01:31,440
Our crews, they were so young.

20
00:01:31,441 --> 00:01:33,720
This was an adventure for them.

21
00:01:33,721 --> 00:01:36,320
We didn't think about the problems connected with them.

22
00:01:36,321 --> 00:01:39,880
We only thought about the target, big, fat targets.

23
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:59,040
At that moment, you wished you were in the infantry or in the army, where you have at

24
00:01:59,041 --> 00:01:59,960
least your foxhole.

25
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:08,440
In the submarine, they are all heroes or cowards.

26
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:09,940
There is no escape.

27
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:15,620
The submarine was just a fledgling weapon when it first went to war in 1914.

28
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:19,040
The German Navy held its surface ships back initially

29
00:02:19,041 --> 00:02:21,680
and ordered its fleet of 20 undersea boats

30
00:02:21,681 --> 00:02:25,880
to stop the flow of raw materials to the island nation of England.

31
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:29,040
The English Navy took little notice.

32
00:02:29,660 --> 00:02:31,200
They had submarines of their own,

33
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:34,280
but few British admirals thought underwater ships

34
00:02:34,281 --> 00:02:37,340
would dare tangle with the big guns of their surface fleet.

35
00:02:38,380 --> 00:02:39,580
Initially in the First World War,

36
00:02:39,581 --> 00:02:42,020
the German Navy were operating their submarines

37
00:02:42,021 --> 00:02:44,220
on a sort of alone wolf basis.

38
00:02:44,221 --> 00:02:46,340
For this, they required good intelligence.

39
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:49,260
They didn't have long-range aircoff to provide this.

40
00:02:49,560 --> 00:02:55,600
They had to rely upon agents reporting the sailings of convoys from New York or Halifax in Nova Scotia,

41
00:02:55,840 --> 00:03:01,700
and they then had to use dead reckoning to try and put themselves into a position where they could intercept these convoys.

42
00:03:01,980 --> 00:03:07,480
They were controlled by wireless, which gave them the latest updates of information that had been received.

43
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:09,460
It was a very hit-and-miss operation.

44
00:03:09,461 --> 00:03:20,380
On September 22, 1914, U-9 was cruising off the Dutch coast when it came upon three 12,000-ton British armored cruisers.

45
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:24,660
Lieutenant Otto Weddegen calmly ordered his sub to dive.

46
00:03:25,220 --> 00:03:30,180
His men had never fired a torpedo in combat, nor had they ever seen a ship blow up.

47
00:03:32,280 --> 00:03:37,120
Within minutes, they prepared the fire control tubes, and the order was given to fire.

48
00:03:39,460 --> 00:03:45,220
In the confusion that followed, all the ships stopped.

49
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:48,660
On board, the crews thought they had stumbled onto a minefield.

50
00:03:49,180 --> 00:03:52,320
As they began to turn around, four more torpedoes were launched.

51
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:59,640
Within one hour, the three ships and 1,460 of their men slipped beneath the surface.

52
00:04:00,460 --> 00:04:02,180
Most never knew what hit them.

53
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:05,020
The attack shocked the British Admiralty.

54
00:04:05,021 --> 00:04:09,620
No one would dispute the submarine's arrival as a viable weapon of war.

55
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:16,420
The U-boats invariably approached on the surface where they had a speed of perhaps 15 knots.

56
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:22,220
Merchant ships in those days did 7, 8, maybe 9 knots if they were fast merchant ships.

57
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:27,140
This meant the U-boat could maneuver into a favorable position.

58
00:04:27,900 --> 00:04:32,800
If the surface ship was unarmed, the U-boat would then come alongside,

59
00:04:33,460 --> 00:04:36,660
order the crew to abandon, sink the ship with its deck gun.

60
00:04:37,620 --> 00:04:41,400
Bullets were relatively cheap. Torpedoes were relatively expensive.

61
00:04:42,220 --> 00:04:45,420
If the ship was armed or there was an escort ship in the area,

62
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:50,040
then the U-boat would submerge just before it came into view of the merchant ship,

63
00:04:50,820 --> 00:04:55,700
fire torpedoes, sink it that way without giving the crew any warning.

64
00:04:56,440 --> 00:05:02,480
The sinkings only bolstered the morale and audacity of the U-boat crews, and they became bolder.

65
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:08,520
Using a deck gun as their primary weapon, they sunk 31 ships for every sub lost.

66
00:05:08,980 --> 00:05:14,460
The losses were so appalling that they had to do something.

67
00:05:14,720 --> 00:05:18,260
The food supplies in Britain in 1917 got down to six weeks.

68
00:05:18,261 --> 00:05:27,980
The German U-boats developed a stranglehold on Britain that was only broken with the American intervention in 1917.

69
00:05:29,060 --> 00:05:33,040
The U.S. and Britain instituted a system of ocean-going convoys.

70
00:05:33,620 --> 00:05:39,580
Cargo ships bound for England sailed close together and relied on destroyer escorts to protect them.

71
00:05:42,120 --> 00:05:47,940
Altogether, the anti-submarine forces sunk 178 submarines, half of the U-boat fleet.

72
00:05:48,260 --> 00:05:50,800
With the convoys came almost complete protection.

73
00:05:51,120 --> 00:05:54,720
The individual U-boats could not get into the protected convoys,

74
00:05:55,300 --> 00:05:58,260
and so they ended that war figuring we've solved the problem.

75
00:05:59,720 --> 00:06:04,460
After the war ended, the Treaty of Versailles directed all remaining German Navy ships

76
00:06:04,461 --> 00:06:06,760
to be turned over to the United Kingdom.

77
00:06:07,520 --> 00:06:12,240
One by one, the German submarines sailed into the Royal Navy anchorage at Scapa Flow.

78
00:06:12,920 --> 00:06:15,120
Within weeks, most of them were scrapped.

79
00:06:15,121 --> 00:06:21,520
Under terms of the treaty, Germany was also forbidden to build or sail any more underwater vessels.

80
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:25,020
German sub-research continued undercover.

81
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:27,820
Working out of a shipbuilding firm in Holland,

82
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,540
German engineers built several submarines for sale to other countries.

83
00:06:32,360 --> 00:06:34,960
When Adolf Hitler came to power in 1933,

84
00:06:34,961 --> 00:06:40,360
he found a corps of U-boat engineers ready for an all-out manufacturing effort.

85
00:06:40,361 --> 00:06:46,940
Two years later, Hitler announced he was throwing off the shackles of Versailles

86
00:06:46,941 --> 00:06:53,080
In June of 1935, he signed a new accord, the Anglo-German Naval Agreement

87
00:06:53,081 --> 00:06:58,780
Germany agreed to maintain its navy at 35% of the strength of the British Royal Navy

88
00:06:58,781 --> 00:07:03,980
Submarines were now permitted to be built to 45% of the British subforce

89
00:07:03,981 --> 00:07:09,140
With the treaty signed, Hitler was satisfied he would have no war with Britain

90
00:07:09,141 --> 00:07:13,560
and turned his attention to rebuilding his Luftwaffe and Panzer divisions.

91
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:17,460
The English, on the other hand, saw little use for submarines.

92
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:22,680
Besides, they were working on ASDIC, an underwater sonar detection system.

93
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:27,340
By 1937, they were so confident of its potential,

94
00:07:27,900 --> 00:07:29,680
the British Shipping Defense Committee said,

95
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:33,540
submarines will never again present a threat to the Royal Navy.

96
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:38,920
Of all the naval exercises carried out by Britain between the wars,

97
00:07:39,140 --> 00:07:42,500
Not one was devoted to the defense of trade convoys.

98
00:07:43,420 --> 00:07:47,240
In the meantime, the German submarine program began to flourish again.

99
00:07:48,060 --> 00:07:52,080
The inspiration behind the development of the German U-boat fleet was Karl Dornitz.

100
00:07:52,081 --> 00:07:58,380
He made considerable steps to improve the safety, for example, escape apparatus for his crews.

101
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:04,000
He also looked at the types and tactics and operations that they would be carrying out in the event of war.

102
00:08:04,001 --> 00:08:11,760
He took the submarines themselves on further than any other single person had done in terms of inspiration.

103
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:19,000
By the end of 1935, 14 U-boats had already materialized in the shipyards at Keele.

104
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:24,180
To hide and protect them, underground submarine pens were built,

105
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:30,980
bolstered by 12-foot concrete ceilings, anti-aircraft guns, and the latest in acoustical airplane sound detectors.

106
00:08:30,981 --> 00:08:35,980
For the submarine service, only volunteers were accepted.

107
00:08:35,981 --> 00:08:41,980
We knew that we had to do our duty for our country.

108
00:08:41,981 --> 00:08:48,980
Perhaps that's in the mentality of Sherman people, especially the youth of that time.

109
00:08:48,981 --> 00:08:55,980
I belonged to the Hitler Youth and it was more being a hero.

110
00:08:55,981 --> 00:09:00,340
And that's what made me volunteer for the Navy.

111
00:09:01,220 --> 00:09:03,820
It was tough. It was congested.

112
00:09:04,520 --> 00:09:07,040
It was dirty. It was hard.

113
00:09:07,980 --> 00:09:12,380
But it also, you were men when you came back from one or two patrols.

114
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:16,140
The Navy was part of the cream already,

115
00:09:16,280 --> 00:09:18,440
but the submarinos were the cream of the Navy.

116
00:09:18,680 --> 00:09:22,180
We had to be very, very smart,

117
00:09:22,181 --> 00:09:27,820
because when we went to school, we had to answer questions in a certain way.

118
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:32,560
And when we got asked for something, we had to answer right away

119
00:09:32,561 --> 00:09:39,400
without taking a minute finding the reason why or what we were asked for.

120
00:09:39,401 --> 00:09:47,540
And that's, I think, a program to select people who were able and who were not able

121
00:09:47,541 --> 00:09:53,800
because a lot of them, after the school or even during the school time,

122
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:56,340
they were sent to other parts of the Navy

123
00:09:56,341 --> 00:09:59,320
because they were not able to join submarine crews.

124
00:10:00,700 --> 00:10:05,720
In 1937, Dönitz organized extensive naval war games in the North Sea

125
00:10:05,721 --> 00:10:07,460
to test a number of his theories.

126
00:10:08,660 --> 00:10:11,360
His men had learned to attack at night for surprise

127
00:10:11,361 --> 00:10:13,640
and on the surface where sonar wasn't effective.

128
00:10:13,641 --> 00:10:22,940
Dönitz also moved the U-boats in from their normal attack range of one and three-quarter miles to just 550 yards from their target.

129
00:10:23,600 --> 00:10:28,420
Now they didn't need to fire a salvo of shots when one well-placed torpedo could do the job.

130
00:10:29,160 --> 00:10:31,860
A torpedo is a ship, a submarine by itself.

131
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:33,760
It's very expensive.

132
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:40,800
In our case, it was always a saying, a torpedo, a ship, which could not always be accomplished.

133
00:10:40,801 --> 00:10:50,220
A torpedo was preset for the distance, for the speed, for the depth, so that they could hit a target broadside.

134
00:10:51,180 --> 00:10:56,300
Early torpedoes were percussion ignited, exploding only on direct contact with a ship.

135
00:10:57,300 --> 00:11:04,140
Later, a magnetic ignition was incorporated that set off the torpedo when it entered the magnetic field of a ship at sea.

136
00:11:04,740 --> 00:11:06,560
Now the depth setting was less critical.

137
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:12,420
The torpedoes would now explode beneath the ship's hull and have a better chance of sinking it.

138
00:11:14,440 --> 00:11:20,240
The Anglo-German treaty restricted the total tonnage of German submarines, but not their amount.

139
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:24,100
Admiral Dönitz maintained there was strength in numbers.

140
00:11:24,760 --> 00:11:29,380
Four small U-boats were far more effective than one four times larger, he said.

141
00:11:30,060 --> 00:11:35,860
The aim of the Germans was that by the beginning of the war, which they expected to be in 1942 rather than 1939,

142
00:11:35,861 --> 00:11:38,500
that they would have some 100 U-boats at sea.

143
00:11:38,900 --> 00:11:42,260
As it was in 1939, they had only a little over 30.

144
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:45,860
But those 30 almost brought the United Kingdom to its knees

145
00:11:45,861 --> 00:11:48,320
when it stood alone against Nazi Germany.

146
00:11:52,160 --> 00:11:55,020
The Navy designed many types of U-boats,

147
00:11:55,240 --> 00:11:57,540
but most were of three classifications.

148
00:11:58,220 --> 00:12:02,200
The small Type II was used for training and coastal patrols

149
00:12:02,201 --> 00:12:03,480
in the Baltic and North Sea.

150
00:12:03,481 --> 00:12:06,040
It weighed around 250 tons.

151
00:12:06,740 --> 00:12:13,560
The Type 9, between 1,100 and 1,400 tons, was designed for long patrols through the Atlantic and beyond.

152
00:12:15,100 --> 00:12:20,680
The medium-sized Type 7 weighed between 500 and 750 tons.

153
00:12:21,320 --> 00:12:26,760
Engineered for long cruises, it was built in far greater numbers than any other submarine in history.

154
00:12:26,761 --> 00:12:33,820
Two 1,400 horsepower diesel engines drove it on the surface at speeds of 17 knots.

155
00:12:34,600 --> 00:12:39,820
The moment she submerged, engineers switched over to two 750 horsepower electric motors

156
00:12:39,821 --> 00:12:42,700
that produced a top speed of 8 knots.

157
00:12:42,701 --> 00:12:56,701
Two days after the Blitzkrieg attack on Poland, Britain declared war on Germany.

158
00:12:58,300 --> 00:13:02,560
46 U-boats were available, but only 22 were designed for long ranges.

159
00:13:03,700 --> 00:13:06,540
Still, they attempted to strangle Britain's supply lines

160
00:13:06,541 --> 00:13:09,940
and return to the same battleground where they had fought 20 years earlier.

161
00:13:12,700 --> 00:13:20,860
On October 14, 1939, the U-boat Corps had perhaps its grandest hour.

162
00:13:21,740 --> 00:13:26,240
Lieutenant Gunther Preen was given the mission to attack the British fleet at Scapa Flow.

163
00:13:27,080 --> 00:13:34,440
The largest of all the Royal Navy harbors, Scapa Flow guarded the exit route of the German Navy from the North Sea.

164
00:13:35,200 --> 00:13:39,320
That night, Lieutenant Preen took his U-47 into the British harbor

165
00:13:39,321 --> 00:13:43,940
and edged past the rusted remains of the scuttled World War I German fleet.

166
00:13:44,640 --> 00:13:48,040
There he found a target, the battleship Royal Oak.

167
00:13:48,740 --> 00:13:51,760
Preen launched a salvo of torpedoes, but they didn't detonate.

168
00:13:52,320 --> 00:13:55,520
Then he ordered more, which exploded under the ship's keel.

169
00:13:56,140 --> 00:14:01,120
The 25-year-old veteran of the Battle of Jutland capsized and sank 13 minutes later,

170
00:14:01,121 --> 00:14:04,040
along with her crew of 833 men.

171
00:14:04,440 --> 00:14:07,640
The U-boat quietly slipped out of the harbor.

172
00:14:08,900 --> 00:14:11,020
It was a great coup for the U-boat force.

173
00:14:11,780 --> 00:14:16,820
It was one of the first great propaganda victories of Germany

174
00:14:16,821 --> 00:14:21,320
because they could point out that even in the home base of the British fleet,

175
00:14:22,380 --> 00:14:23,920
U-boats could easily penetrate.

176
00:14:24,840 --> 00:14:27,800
And the British were forced to move the fleet out of Scapa Flow,

177
00:14:28,040 --> 00:14:29,360
rebuild the defenses,

178
00:14:29,361 --> 00:14:34,680
and it really hurt their ability to rapidly deploy into the North Sea.

179
00:14:35,760 --> 00:14:39,580
Each month, Britain was dependent on 1,000 cargo ships

180
00:14:39,581 --> 00:14:42,360
to bring it the food and raw materials to survive.

181
00:14:43,280 --> 00:14:46,780
As First Lord of the Admiralty, Churchill armed his merchantmen

182
00:14:46,781 --> 00:14:49,140
and reinstituted the convoy system.

183
00:14:50,680 --> 00:14:53,640
Spring of 1940, when Germany defeated France,

184
00:14:54,360 --> 00:14:58,060
the decision was made to base the U-boats in French ports.

185
00:14:58,061 --> 00:15:03,340
The reason for this was it would save a lot of transit time from German ports

186
00:15:03,341 --> 00:15:07,980
Plus the U-boats would not have to go around the coast of Scotland and Britain

187
00:15:07,981 --> 00:15:12,480
Where they would be more vulnerable to British anti-submarine forces

188
00:15:12,481 --> 00:15:19,420
One of the interesting things that they had to help them was the long-range Focke-Wulf Condor bomber

189
00:15:19,421 --> 00:15:23,160
This aircraft converted for maritime reconnaissance

190
00:15:23,161 --> 00:15:27,360
flying from bases in France, was able to pinpoint convoys

191
00:15:27,361 --> 00:15:29,720
and radio that information to the U-boats.

192
00:15:30,360 --> 00:15:33,840
I suppose really the Condor was one of the most serious risks

193
00:15:33,841 --> 00:15:38,100
to convoy operations and one of the greatest assets to a U-boat.

194
00:15:39,760 --> 00:15:44,080
The happy time is how the submariners described the summer of 1940.

195
00:15:44,940 --> 00:15:47,080
In June, 58 ships were sunk.

196
00:15:47,640 --> 00:15:49,340
July saw 38 sunk.

197
00:15:49,820 --> 00:15:50,960
August, 56.

198
00:15:50,961 --> 00:15:54,780
all with the loss of just one U-boat.

199
00:15:56,280 --> 00:16:01,260
The convoys were consisted of about 40, 50 ships

200
00:16:01,261 --> 00:16:05,420
and they usually drove in four lines

201
00:16:05,421 --> 00:16:08,360
surrounded by destroyers.

202
00:16:09,160 --> 00:16:11,140
The mishap of a convoys over there,

203
00:16:11,780 --> 00:16:14,100
they have to regulate their speed

204
00:16:14,101 --> 00:16:16,760
according to the slowest of their ships

205
00:16:16,761 --> 00:16:20,940
and that brought them sometimes down to 8 miles an hour.

206
00:16:20,960 --> 00:16:25,560
The convoys called for both discipline and patience.

207
00:16:26,220 --> 00:16:30,080
To compensate for their slow speeds, the convoys changed course frequently,

208
00:16:30,540 --> 00:16:32,400
zigzagging their way across the ocean.

209
00:16:33,420 --> 00:16:37,820
In October 1940, a convoy of 35 ships left Newfoundland.

210
00:16:38,620 --> 00:16:40,240
Only 14 made it to England.

211
00:16:41,480 --> 00:16:44,620
To compound the problem, U.S. shipping insurance companies

212
00:16:44,621 --> 00:16:48,000
continued to share their risks with European underwriters

213
00:16:48,001 --> 00:16:52,900
and freely corresponded with information detailing freighter cargo and sailing dates.

214
00:16:53,480 --> 00:16:56,920
These details routinely fell into the hands of German naval intelligence.

215
00:16:57,560 --> 00:17:03,460
For the first three years of the war, the Germans had free access to our sailing instruction,

216
00:17:03,960 --> 00:17:08,580
the composition of our convoys, and it makes my hair stand on the end now to think

217
00:17:08,581 --> 00:17:14,100
that with all the secrecy we had, that the Germans had the information almost as fast as we did.

218
00:17:14,101 --> 00:17:20,840
The U-boat skippers always left their ports with sealed orders under the cover of darkness

219
00:17:20,841 --> 00:17:26,420
When the U-boats went to sea, they were going out for a 30, 60 day patrol

220
00:17:26,421 --> 00:17:32,820
Every bit of space in the U-boat was filled with supplies

221
00:17:32,821 --> 00:17:36,080
Some ammunition, but mostly food

222
00:17:36,081 --> 00:17:39,660
U-boats had two small bathrooms or heads

223
00:17:39,661 --> 00:17:42,860
When they went to sea, one was stuffed with food

224
00:17:42,861 --> 00:17:54,560
The decks inside the submarine were covered with food, and then planks put over the cans and the bags, and the men walked on the planks the first few weeks.

225
00:17:55,560 --> 00:17:59,720
Of course, there was no air conditioning. The submarine stank from diesel oil.

226
00:18:00,360 --> 00:18:07,720
Because of the limited amount of water that they could distill and that they could carry, showers were rarely taken.

227
00:18:07,721 --> 00:18:15,680
We only could wash ourselves out of a small pail, but not with fresh water.

228
00:18:15,760 --> 00:18:16,920
We had to use seawater.

229
00:18:17,680 --> 00:18:24,460
And seawater, as you know, after a certain time, when the water is absorbed, there's only salt left.

230
00:18:24,680 --> 00:18:26,580
And that salt is getting sticky.

231
00:18:28,060 --> 00:18:34,600
Half of the crew bunked in the bow section, an area no wider than 12 feet across, and slept next to the torpedoes.

232
00:18:34,601 --> 00:18:37,980
In the submarine itself, you always hot bunked.

233
00:18:38,200 --> 00:18:40,520
That is, there were bunks for maybe half the crew.

234
00:18:41,140 --> 00:18:45,600
So if a man was on duty, there was someone in his bunk, his bunkmate.

235
00:18:46,160 --> 00:18:50,420
When he got off duty, the bunkmate went on duty and he got the bunk.

236
00:18:50,580 --> 00:18:52,320
Nice and warm and a little smelling.

237
00:18:53,340 --> 00:18:58,960
Every crewman had the right to leave his U-boat as soon as a replacement could be found, no questions asked.

238
00:18:58,961 --> 00:19:03,420
being locked in a steel tube with 30 or 40 other guys

239
00:19:03,421 --> 00:19:08,140
for 30 or 60 days, psychologically quite a problem.

240
00:19:08,840 --> 00:19:10,680
They underwent psychological testing,

241
00:19:11,380 --> 00:19:14,460
and most of their officers and skippers were outstanding

242
00:19:14,461 --> 00:19:17,680
to keep the boat under control.

243
00:19:17,681 --> 00:19:22,580
And then when the crisis came of being attacked or attacking,

244
00:19:22,980 --> 00:19:26,560
to get the crew to work together and to perform,

245
00:19:26,561 --> 00:19:28,960
in some cases magnificently.

246
00:19:39,100 --> 00:19:41,880
The moment the torpedo hit, there was a great relief

247
00:19:41,881 --> 00:19:44,000
because you have been out there for weeks.

248
00:19:44,480 --> 00:19:46,180
You have been trying to find somebody.

249
00:19:46,700 --> 00:19:49,940
And this from day to day looking and trying to find

250
00:19:49,941 --> 00:19:51,300
is wearing you down.

251
00:19:51,720 --> 00:19:55,420
In a submarine patrol, there are many, many days

252
00:19:55,421 --> 00:19:58,340
where you're just out there searching and trying to find,

253
00:19:58,640 --> 00:20:02,320
and that ocean is so big, and you're so small,

254
00:20:02,420 --> 00:20:05,000
and you have such a short area of seeing something.

255
00:20:05,960 --> 00:20:09,860
The boat would go to a certain speed to conserve diesel oil,

256
00:20:10,320 --> 00:20:11,500
and that would be a slow speed.

257
00:20:11,820 --> 00:20:15,140
If all at once the engine picks up, there must be something.

258
00:20:15,240 --> 00:20:17,760
There must be a target, or somebody must have seen somebody.

259
00:20:17,980 --> 00:20:20,140
So out of the Bronx, every head comes out.

260
00:20:20,240 --> 00:20:21,340
What's going on? What's going on?

261
00:20:21,341 --> 00:20:23,860
We have changed. We have increased our speed.

262
00:20:23,861 --> 00:20:26,580
if there's anything, and then you try to find out.

263
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,940
So then when you really have a target,

264
00:20:30,120 --> 00:20:32,960
and then if you really shoot at the target, there's a relief.

265
00:20:33,920 --> 00:20:38,020
The German U-boat men, the Kriegsmarine, had two kinds of uniforms.

266
00:20:38,560 --> 00:20:42,340
Blue for men who served in the boat, and gray for men who served ashore.

267
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,080
U-boats will return in a moment on modern models.

268
00:20:46,380 --> 00:20:48,500
After the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor,

269
00:20:48,920 --> 00:20:51,080
the course of the war was unalterable,

270
00:20:51,081 --> 00:20:54,080
and Hitler ordered his U-boats to American waters.

271
00:20:55,100 --> 00:20:58,500
The U-boats would lie on the shallow ocean floor by day

272
00:20:58,501 --> 00:21:00,280
and rise to the surface at night.

273
00:21:00,620 --> 00:21:06,800
I saw only the cars driving with the headlights still on.

274
00:21:07,100 --> 00:21:08,460
But they didn't expect us.

275
00:21:09,080 --> 00:21:11,200
They could not, nobody in the world,

276
00:21:11,920 --> 00:21:16,440
all the skilled people of the Navy and of the submarine forces,

277
00:21:16,960 --> 00:21:20,240
they could not imagine that the Germans could accomplish

278
00:21:20,241 --> 00:21:26,020
to come to the cross the whole Atlantic, fight there, and still have enough fuel to go back.

279
00:21:27,080 --> 00:21:32,160
We kept the lights on in early 1942 on our East Coast cities.

280
00:21:32,720 --> 00:21:38,240
Part of the reason was our coastal shipping needed the lights to navigate along the coast.

281
00:21:38,740 --> 00:21:41,860
But of course, these were also beacons for German submarines.

282
00:21:43,060 --> 00:21:46,400
With the lights of New York, Cape Hatteras, and Miami behind them,

283
00:21:46,740 --> 00:21:49,460
the American ships were perfectly silhouetted at night.

284
00:21:50,240 --> 00:22:01,200
The German submarines were just killing our convoys, especially the tankers coming out of Venezuela, trying to get to ports in New Orleans.

285
00:22:01,680 --> 00:22:07,360
They would just lay in wait for those tankers to come out of there and then just have a field day.

286
00:22:07,560 --> 00:22:10,000
They just used them for target practice.

287
00:22:11,180 --> 00:22:16,860
At the maximum, during the next six months, the Germans never had more than 12 submarines on the station.

288
00:22:16,861 --> 00:22:20,420
and this was about 600 million men in those submarines.

289
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
Yet, in that six months period,

290
00:22:23,800 --> 00:22:28,480
they sank two and a half million tons of shipping right along that coast.

291
00:22:28,580 --> 00:22:31,520
You could see the ship burning off from the beaches.

292
00:22:32,720 --> 00:22:35,860
Along the coast, U.S. forces mobilized.

293
00:22:36,580 --> 00:22:39,960
Major harbors were lined with steel anti-submarine nets

294
00:22:39,961 --> 00:22:41,920
and patrolled by torpedo boats.

295
00:22:42,600 --> 00:22:44,120
With Navy blimps overhead

296
00:22:44,121 --> 00:22:47,180
and thousands of volunteer private yachts below.

297
00:22:47,940 --> 00:22:49,520
All eyes were on the lookout.

298
00:22:50,940 --> 00:22:52,900
As the number of U-boats grew,

299
00:22:53,460 --> 00:22:55,780
their skippers began to concentrate their attacks

300
00:22:55,781 --> 00:22:56,820
in the middle of the Atlantic,

301
00:22:57,020 --> 00:23:00,500
where long-range surveillance aircraft couldn't patrol for long.

302
00:23:01,560 --> 00:23:04,820
Here, the U-boats executed Admiral Dernit's master strategy,

303
00:23:05,280 --> 00:23:08,020
attacking in groups known as wolf packs.

304
00:23:08,580 --> 00:23:11,620
Each of the boats, when we were out,

305
00:23:11,621 --> 00:23:25,621
10, 12 boats, later on 15 boats, had a square to go up and down with low speed, one propeller, 12 hours south and the same distance, 12 hours north.

306
00:23:28,200 --> 00:23:36,740
When one of the boats got connection with a convoy, then it was not permitted to attack.

307
00:23:37,600 --> 00:23:41,420
But it gave this information to headquarters in Paris.

308
00:23:41,940 --> 00:23:45,760
And they ordered then the other boats to that position.

309
00:23:47,320 --> 00:23:52,140
Running at 17 knots on the surface, the U-boats could outpace the convoys.

310
00:23:52,141 --> 00:23:57,180
We saw the smoke of the convoys

311
00:23:57,181 --> 00:23:59,760
And then we approached them closer

312
00:23:59,761 --> 00:24:03,100
Till we saw the masts and the funnel

313
00:24:03,101 --> 00:24:06,760
But never that close that we saw their bridge

314
00:24:06,761 --> 00:24:09,800
Because when we saw their bridge

315
00:24:09,801 --> 00:24:11,780
They were able to see us too

316
00:24:11,781 --> 00:24:15,940
Once the U-boats met, they waited until dark

317
00:24:15,941 --> 00:24:18,340
Five, six, seven or eight submarines

318
00:24:18,341 --> 00:24:20,120
Were coordinated by a leader

319
00:24:20,121 --> 00:24:27,540
It stationed perhaps two submarines ahead of the convoy, two to attack from the side, and one to catch stragglers from behind.

320
00:24:28,540 --> 00:24:35,840
They could use decoy operations, and one submarine attacking the escorts and drawing them off, or the other submarines attack the merchant ships.

321
00:24:35,841 --> 00:24:49,841
probably the worst moments were when you knew there was no help coming you were going to fight

322
00:24:49,900 --> 00:24:56,740
it out as you were and you had four or five ships burning at one time a radio man or the

323
00:24:56,741 --> 00:25:01,360
engine personnel they are down in the boat they don't see anything there's only about a dozen

324
00:25:01,361 --> 00:25:04,631
of people in any submarine who see what's going on.

325
00:25:04,632 --> 00:25:06,940
But once the Commandant Buchenberger

326
00:25:06,941 --> 00:25:11,818
let me up at the bridge for a few minutes. I never forget that picture.

327
00:25:11,819 --> 00:25:12,780
It's ingrained

328
00:25:12,781 --> 00:25:13,623
in my head.

329
00:25:13,624 --> 00:25:19,680
What I saw there was a large tanker with a flame as high as the tanker was long

330
00:25:19,681 --> 00:25:20,397
above that.

331
00:25:20,398 --> 00:25:25,420
And I know what I felt, and I felt great pity for the sailors on that tanker.

332
00:25:25,421 --> 00:25:34,720
In 1941, the submarines made 445 ships disappear at a cost of only 38 of their own.

333
00:25:35,780 --> 00:25:38,260
The commercial shipping business was devastated.

334
00:25:39,040 --> 00:25:43,340
Lloyd's of London Insurance bookkeepers worked overtime calculating their losses.

335
00:25:44,620 --> 00:25:52,260
A new torpedo was introduced in 1942 that gave the U-boats greater protection and even more firepower.

336
00:25:53,080 --> 00:25:56,940
The loot torpedoes traveled in a straight line for a predetermined distance

337
00:25:56,941 --> 00:26:00,280
and then zigzagged in spirals and figure-eight patterns.

338
00:26:00,940 --> 00:26:04,180
Inside a convoy, it had a greater chance of hitting a ship.

339
00:26:05,520 --> 00:26:12,340
In 1942, another type of U-boat appeared, type 14, known as the milk cow.

340
00:26:12,740 --> 00:26:17,960
This long-range tanker carried 700 tons of diesel fuel and other supplies.

341
00:26:18,720 --> 00:26:21,140
When U-boats rendezvoused with them in the middle of the Atlantic,

342
00:26:21,141 --> 00:26:24,620
they were replenished with fuel, food and more ammunition.

343
00:26:25,340 --> 00:26:29,020
For some, this increased their cruise time up to eight weeks.

344
00:26:30,880 --> 00:26:33,360
With penance that showed their conquests,

345
00:26:33,880 --> 00:26:36,800
the U-boat cruise returned home to a hero's welcome.

346
00:26:42,300 --> 00:26:44,400
Oh, there was always music there.

347
00:26:44,520 --> 00:26:45,420
There were bands there.

348
00:26:45,500 --> 00:26:47,000
There were girls there with flowers.

349
00:26:47,640 --> 00:26:50,500
There was somebody there to give you a beer or another drink.

350
00:26:50,501 --> 00:26:53,960
and there was cheering, a lot of cheering,

351
00:26:54,200 --> 00:26:58,560
and then usually one of the admirals or even donuts would receive you

352
00:26:58,561 --> 00:27:01,040
and then you got your decorations

353
00:27:01,041 --> 00:27:08,320
and then you were able to get a shower, a shave, feel clean again,

354
00:27:08,980 --> 00:27:10,960
smell better than before.

355
00:27:12,600 --> 00:27:14,120
So it was a nice feeling to be home.

356
00:27:14,121 --> 00:27:18,680
And of course when you're home, you were safe once more.

357
00:27:18,681 --> 00:27:23,020
You had life with you once more.

358
00:27:23,021 --> 00:27:28,120
Although never seeing any action, the Walter U-boat was able to reach a speed of 23 knots.

359
00:27:28,121 --> 00:27:32,680
This is more than twice the underwater speed of any submarine in the world at that time.

360
00:27:32,681 --> 00:27:38,500
Today, the Soviet Alpha-class fast attack submarines are able to reach 42 knots.

361
00:27:38,501 --> 00:27:42,800
U-boats will return in a moment on Modern Marvels.

362
00:27:42,801 --> 00:27:49,800
In 1942, German submarines had sunk 1,094 freighters.

363
00:27:49,801 --> 00:27:54,800
By December, Dernitz had at his disposal 397 submarines,

364
00:27:54,801 --> 00:27:57,800
nearly 100 more than he had originally hoped for.

365
00:27:57,801 --> 00:28:03,800
In 1943, Britain was within a couple of weeks of being starved out of the war.

366
00:28:03,801 --> 00:28:10,800
The German submarine attacks on convoys were so successful in 1943

367
00:28:10,801 --> 00:28:15,160
that the British considered giving up convoys,

368
00:28:15,480 --> 00:28:18,860
and yet convoys was what had saved them in World War I.

369
00:28:19,520 --> 00:28:21,980
But the U-boat was that much more effective.

370
00:28:22,860 --> 00:28:26,060
But the Wolfpack strategy had its weaknesses as well.

371
00:28:26,540 --> 00:28:29,980
It put too much responsibility on the lone shadowing U-boat.

372
00:28:29,981 --> 00:28:33,720
When the convoys began organizing air cover behind them,

373
00:28:34,080 --> 00:28:35,680
the submarine was forced to dive,

374
00:28:36,020 --> 00:28:38,820
and at slower speeds underwater couldn't keep up.

375
00:28:38,821 --> 00:28:43,220
The centralized command of the U-boats was their greatest liability.

376
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:47,800
In order to transmit their positions, they had to break radio silence.

377
00:28:48,540 --> 00:28:53,740
Unknown to the U-boats, their transmissions were being picked up by several Allied shore stations.

378
00:28:54,540 --> 00:28:58,900
Working in unison, the stations homed in on the direction of the radio messages

379
00:28:58,901 --> 00:29:01,020
and made a fix of their position.

380
00:29:01,220 --> 00:29:06,100
The convoys were rerouted, and the nearest destroyers were dispatched to the area.

381
00:29:08,820 --> 00:29:13,300
In May of 1941, the British perfected an ocean-going surface radar

382
00:29:13,301 --> 00:29:16,640
that could detect a conning tower from two and a half miles away.

383
00:29:17,500 --> 00:29:21,920
Radar meant that the U-boat could not easily surface without being detected.

384
00:29:22,840 --> 00:29:25,460
Radar could sight them in rain, in darkness,

385
00:29:26,140 --> 00:29:28,600
force them to remain submerged even at night.

386
00:29:29,260 --> 00:29:31,840
This really deprived the U-boats of their mobility.

387
00:29:33,020 --> 00:29:37,700
With the range of air cover gradually being extended on both sides of the Atlantic,

388
00:29:37,701 --> 00:29:42,000
the air gap in the middle was shortened down to only 600 miles.

389
00:29:42,920 --> 00:29:45,880
Dernitz then decided to pull his U-boats to north.

390
00:29:47,240 --> 00:29:50,740
The war in the Atlantic took a dramatic turn in 1943.

391
00:29:51,860 --> 00:29:56,740
In March, we lost nearly 100 ships,

392
00:29:57,400 --> 00:30:01,400
and the losses among the crews were terrible because of the storms.

393
00:30:01,700 --> 00:30:03,100
You didn't recover many of them.

394
00:30:03,101 --> 00:30:07,960
Then the greatest convoy battle of all time was fought in the middle of March.

395
00:30:08,100 --> 00:30:09,760
That was a combined convoy.

396
00:30:10,340 --> 00:30:23,880
Within 100 miles, either way, 100 by 100 mile area, you had 80 merchant ships, 20 men of war, 44 submarines and innumerable liberators and sunderlandmen.

397
00:30:23,881 --> 00:30:30,140
And everybody was milling around in that small section of ocean with their guns and torpedoes ready.

398
00:30:30,580 --> 00:30:32,460
And it was a wild, fierce battle.

399
00:30:32,461 --> 00:30:44,880
in April the convoys managed to drive off all attacking subs in May 42 U-boats were sunk up

400
00:30:44,881 --> 00:30:52,560
from 15 the previous month from then on the U-boats were on the defensive the major anti-submarine

401
00:30:52,561 --> 00:30:59,520
warfare weapon for circus ships in the second world war was the depth charge basically uh

402
00:30:59,521 --> 00:31:06,140
explosive TNT packed into a container with a barista pressure system that would be set prior to its launch

403
00:31:06,280 --> 00:31:09,500
If you thought the submarine was at 100 feet you'd set it to explode

404
00:31:10,020 --> 00:31:17,080
Perhaps at 120 feet and the next pattern to explode at 90 feet in order to try and bracket the submarine

405
00:31:21,020 --> 00:31:28,420
You can hear the splash of the depth charges when they hit the surface and then we counted six seconds and

406
00:31:28,421 --> 00:31:31,420
and then you heard the explosion.

407
00:31:32,120 --> 00:31:34,520
As long as they were above us or sideways,

408
00:31:34,940 --> 00:31:36,200
it was not dangerous or deadly,

409
00:31:36,720 --> 00:31:38,640
but it was a terrible feeling.

410
00:31:39,300 --> 00:31:41,940
The worst, what you can do is stand there,

411
00:31:42,840 --> 00:31:44,960
looking upwards, listening,

412
00:31:45,380 --> 00:31:47,340
and waiting for the next step charge.

413
00:31:48,360 --> 00:31:50,360
That drives everybody crazy.

414
00:31:58,420 --> 00:32:01,540
Dead charges is not a very nice feeling.

415
00:32:02,120 --> 00:32:06,660
Your boat is shaking, lights are breaking, people are getting gray in their face,

416
00:32:07,120 --> 00:32:09,760
and nobody is saying in the world you can only whisper.

417
00:32:10,160 --> 00:32:13,520
Not even that, if possible, because you don't want to make any sound.

418
00:32:14,020 --> 00:32:18,220
Any sound travels on the water better than in the air.

419
00:32:19,260 --> 00:32:25,200
The only defense the U-boats had during a depth charge attack was to dive deeper than the explosions.

420
00:32:25,201 --> 00:32:30,240
As the boat submerged, however, the external pressure increased rapidly,

421
00:32:30,440 --> 00:32:34,340
testing the limits to which a U-boat commander would dare take his ship.

422
00:32:35,100 --> 00:32:39,680
At 500 feet, the water pressure was 16 tons per square foot.

423
00:32:40,560 --> 00:32:45,940
Nobody knows how much deeper you can go because those ones who reached deeper depths,

424
00:32:46,500 --> 00:32:47,460
they cannot tell anymore.

425
00:32:48,040 --> 00:32:49,540
They were killed. They were pressed together.

426
00:32:49,541 --> 00:32:55,940
U-boats countered with a sensor that detected Allied radar signals.

427
00:32:56,440 --> 00:32:59,680
Anti-aircraft guns were installed in the spring of 1943.

428
00:33:01,100 --> 00:33:07,820
In 1941, the first of a new kind of radar set was installed in aircraft of the British Coastal Command.

429
00:33:08,520 --> 00:33:13,620
Small and powerful, the air-to-surface radar could locate U-boats once they had surfaced.

430
00:33:14,080 --> 00:33:17,340
Its only drawback was its failure to function at close range.

431
00:33:17,341 --> 00:33:24,860
During the final stages of an approach, the contact faded, and only a visual sighting could pinpoint the U-boat's position.

432
00:33:25,620 --> 00:33:29,800
At night, the U-boats with anti-aircraft guns were still immune to some degree.

433
00:33:31,400 --> 00:33:35,220
The British remedied this situation with the Lee light.

434
00:33:35,920 --> 00:33:38,620
With this light, the British developed a new set of tactics.

435
00:33:38,621 --> 00:33:45,620
They would use radar to generally approach the submarine, then turn off the radar and

436
00:33:45,621 --> 00:33:50,500
turn off their engines and glide toward the submarine.

437
00:33:50,501 --> 00:33:55,900
This way the submarine's radar detection device would not pick up the aircraft.

438
00:33:55,901 --> 00:33:58,180
They wouldn't hear the aircraft.

439
00:33:58,181 --> 00:34:03,900
Then when the pilot estimated he was at the right distance from the surface U-boat, would

440
00:34:03,901 --> 00:34:05,760
switch on this giant strobe light,

441
00:34:07,160 --> 00:34:10,840
totally destroy the night vision of the people on the bridge who were manning the guns.

442
00:34:11,960 --> 00:34:14,780
And in those few seconds, he had a field day.

443
00:34:17,000 --> 00:34:23,780
The victory of radar in the summer of 1943 made it clear that the U-boats would have to be removed from the surface

444
00:34:23,781 --> 00:34:26,440
or give up the battle of the Atlantic completely.

445
00:34:27,280 --> 00:34:33,300
In an effort to overcome this, the Germans started fitting what was called a snorkel or snort to their submarines.

446
00:34:33,900 --> 00:34:38,340
This was literally a breathing tube invented by the Dutch in the 30s

447
00:34:39,060 --> 00:34:44,040
Germans took it over during the war and their submarines could then steam along

448
00:34:44,340 --> 00:34:49,660
Underwater with the snorkel raised run their diesel engines to charge their batteries

449
00:34:51,720 --> 00:34:59,460
But the snorkels presented more problems than they solved in rough weather the valves would shut creating a vacuum in the fresh air

450
00:34:59,461 --> 00:35:00,688
tube for the crew.

451
00:35:00,689 --> 00:35:05,260
Every time the snorkel would open or close or is overrun by a wave,

452
00:35:05,780 --> 00:35:08,815
you would get some water into the intake.

453
00:35:08,816 --> 00:35:12,220
Your ears try to jump out of your head or your

454
00:35:12,221 --> 00:35:15,457
eardrums try to jump out of your head.

455
00:35:15,458 --> 00:35:19,460
And it also has a shocking effect on your eyes.

456
00:35:20,860 --> 00:35:27,160
By 1944, the Allies produced a radar set capable of detecting even the small head of the snorkel.

457
00:35:29,460 --> 00:35:41,660
By the end of 1943, Germany had 442 operational U-boats, but it also lost 245.

458
00:35:42,760 --> 00:35:49,120
The Hedgehog Weapon throws 24 small projectiles several hundred feet ahead of the attacking vessel.

459
00:35:49,880 --> 00:35:55,940
Immediately upon entering the water, the 32-pound projectiles arm and explode on contact with the U-boat.

460
00:35:56,680 --> 00:35:58,960
U-boats will return in a moment on Modern Models.

461
00:35:59,460 --> 00:36:13,240
In June 1944, the USS Guadalcanal and her submarine hunter killer group were on patrol off the coast of Africa when the destroyer Chatelaine picked up a possible sound

462
00:36:13,241 --> 00:36:13,941
contact.

463
00:36:16,160 --> 00:36:20,480
Two wildcats overhead confirmed the presence of a submarine below.

464
00:36:21,340 --> 00:36:25,820
Within minutes, the Chatelaine began throwing depth charges at the target area.

465
00:36:29,460 --> 00:36:34,520
The submarine U-505 sustained a close hit, which jammed her rudder.

466
00:36:35,480 --> 00:36:37,740
The ship's captain gave the order to abort.

467
00:36:39,900 --> 00:36:43,660
Don Carter was a signalman aboard the USS Guadalcanal.

468
00:36:44,140 --> 00:36:47,960
In that vast Atlantic, consider how big it is.

469
00:36:49,120 --> 00:36:54,500
And there we were, just run up on one another, right in the middle of the destroyer escort.

470
00:36:55,620 --> 00:36:58,340
They had them surrounded when they come up.

471
00:36:59,460 --> 00:37:03,060
Three destroyers surrounded U-505 as it broke the surface.

472
00:37:03,560 --> 00:37:05,260
It was pelted from all angles.

473
00:37:05,780 --> 00:37:10,680
Small caliber anti-aircraft fire and .50 caliber machine guns from the Wildcats overhead.

474
00:37:13,180 --> 00:37:15,300
If the order to abort was given,

475
00:37:16,020 --> 00:37:21,520
it was standard procedure for all U-boat crew members to get out of their sub and into rubber rafts.

476
00:37:22,160 --> 00:37:24,980
Three designated men would then set delayed explosives,

477
00:37:25,220 --> 00:37:28,260
giving them 15 minutes to leave the sub and get away.

478
00:37:29,460 --> 00:37:36,440
One of three crew members left on board, Hans Gerbler, pulled a plug on the main seacock, and water began rushing in.

479
00:37:37,440 --> 00:37:41,240
I was wanting to make sure that that boat really went down.

480
00:37:42,100 --> 00:37:54,460
I took the cover from a sea strainer, that's the filter from the main valve, so-called bottom valve, off and threw it in the corner.

481
00:37:54,461 --> 00:38:04,960
But if I would have known that the enemy had in his mind entering that sub, I would have thrown it in the belch.

482
00:38:06,280 --> 00:38:13,100
As soon as Captain Gallery in the Carriaguala Canal realized what was happening, he ordered the submarine to be captured.

483
00:38:14,860 --> 00:38:21,360
Not since 1815 had an American boarding party captured an enemy man of war on the high seas.

484
00:38:21,361 --> 00:38:28,320
We were sure that the boat would disappear from the service within a couple of more minutes.

485
00:38:29,140 --> 00:38:36,780
All of a sudden, we saw whale boats coming from one of them destroyers toward the sub,

486
00:38:36,960 --> 00:38:40,500
and we thought them guys must not be right in their head doing that

487
00:38:40,501 --> 00:38:45,960
because that's something as chumming in a burning fire.

488
00:38:45,961 --> 00:38:54,040
I myself did not even know that the demolition charges were not set in action.

489
00:38:55,580 --> 00:39:00,940
So I was waiting for the moment that the U-505 was blown in pieces.

490
00:39:01,740 --> 00:39:06,620
The first Americans to board the U-505 were from the USS Pillsbury.

491
00:39:08,100 --> 00:39:13,880
Don Carter's boarding party arrived shortly afterwards and began securing a tow rope to the stricken sub.

492
00:39:14,840 --> 00:39:20,600
The only thing that I thought about when I set my foot on the decks of that submarine,

493
00:39:21,200 --> 00:39:23,720
that we had guys going down that conning tower,

494
00:39:24,240 --> 00:39:27,840
that somebody would open one of them doors and it'd be booby-trapped

495
00:39:27,841 --> 00:39:30,940
and we'd get blown about nine miles in the air.

496
00:39:31,900 --> 00:39:38,740
Nothing happened, and that made us feel in some way very, very sad and very bad

497
00:39:38,741 --> 00:39:47,200
because we knew that we were the only German submarine captured by the U.S. Navy.

498
00:39:48,380 --> 00:39:53,140
They got the seacock closed, which kept the water from coming in.

499
00:39:53,560 --> 00:39:59,460
So they went forward and raided the captain's office, got the code book.

500
00:39:59,960 --> 00:40:05,160
That code book gave away every secret that the German submarines had.

501
00:40:05,161 --> 00:40:08,260
They could decode their messages.

502
00:40:08,500 --> 00:40:10,140
They knew where they were in port.

503
00:40:11,660 --> 00:40:13,880
The code books were flown to Washington,

504
00:40:14,100 --> 00:40:16,080
and Captain Gallery called his crew together.

505
00:40:16,480 --> 00:40:20,800
He told us how important it was to keep this a secret

506
00:40:20,801 --> 00:40:23,980
because of all the information that we had got.

507
00:40:24,580 --> 00:40:30,340
Captain Gallery told us that if anybody ever talked about this,

508
00:40:30,880 --> 00:40:33,520
they would eventually find out who he was,

509
00:40:33,521 --> 00:40:35,840
and there would be no trial.

510
00:40:35,960 --> 00:40:39,300
There'd be a court-martial shot at sunrise.

511
00:40:40,320 --> 00:40:44,760
So, and that put the fear of God in everybody.

512
00:40:46,300 --> 00:40:50,540
They said it was the best-kept sacred of World War II.

513
00:40:52,100 --> 00:40:55,520
The last two years, you had hunter-killer groups,

514
00:40:55,900 --> 00:40:58,260
plenty of air to cover, a lot of escorts,

515
00:40:58,261 --> 00:41:00,400
so it was a slaughter.

516
00:41:00,401 --> 00:41:05,080
The Germans lost 785 U-boats during the war.

517
00:41:05,740 --> 00:41:09,940
And probably at no time in recorded military history

518
00:41:09,941 --> 00:41:14,380
has a force kept its morale up so well

519
00:41:14,381 --> 00:41:16,340
in the face of such appalling losses.

520
00:41:17,640 --> 00:41:22,000
On May 4th, 1945, Dönitz ordered all U-boats home.

521
00:41:22,640 --> 00:41:24,500
After his surrender to Allied forces,

522
00:41:24,920 --> 00:41:27,720
he signaled for them to surface and surrender to the British.

523
00:41:28,060 --> 00:41:30,060
His last transmission read,

524
00:41:30,400 --> 00:41:32,560
You have all fought like lions.

525
00:41:33,280 --> 00:41:35,980
Now I must give you the hardest order of your career.

526
00:41:36,660 --> 00:41:42,220
Arise to the surface, hoist a large black flag, and surrender to the enemy.

527
00:41:43,540 --> 00:41:48,740
For the second time in 30 years, the U-boats failed in their bid to defeat the world's navies

528
00:41:48,741 --> 00:41:52,440
and were destined to finish their careers in enemy ports.

529
00:42:00,400 --> 00:42:09,260
Intact, left much the way it was after its capture, U-505 stands in mute testimony to the memory of her fallen comrades.

530
00:42:10,540 --> 00:42:12,560
They are still here, in a sense.

531
00:42:13,480 --> 00:42:19,480
But ironically, they share the same fate as those ships they were designed to defeat.

